# UI rules

All UI / Avalonia / ViewModel conventions live in this file. When adding a new UI-related rule, append a section here — do not create a new `.mdc` file.

## Thin UI

- ViewModels bind and call Business services only — no `AppDbContext`, EF, or SQL.
- No business calculations or validation orchestration in ViewModels (call Business; show results/dialogs).
- UI may depend on DataAccess only for thin startup wiring (DI, `MigrateAsync` at launch).
- All Avalonia UI (Views, dialogs, styles, converters) lives in `CoperativeTelouet.UI` only — never in Business/DataAccess/Domain.
- Prefer XAML Views for dialogs/screens; keep `IUserDialogService` as a thin UI-layer orchestrator that opens those Views.

## One ViewModel / one View per file

- Exactly **one** ViewModel class per `.cs` file (name matches type).
- Exactly **one** View per `.axaml` (+ matching `.axaml.cs` code-behind only).
- Keep 1:1 pairs: `FooViewModel` ↔ `FooView` under matching `ViewModels/` and `Views/` folders.
- Split host/list/detail into separate files (`ClientsViewModel`, `ClientsListViewModel`, `ClientDetailViewModel`).
- Don't put multiple ViewModels or multiple Views in one file.
- Don't embed a full second screen in another view's markup — compose via host + `ContentControl`.
- Shared UI types (enums, converters): **one public type per file**.

```
ViewModels/Vente/Clients/ClientsListViewModel.cs   ✅
Views/Vente/Clients/ClientsListView.axaml          ✅
// ❌ three ViewModels in one ClientsViewModel.cs
```

## Dialogs after database write operations

Every UI action that writes/mutates the database must show a dialog via `IUserDialogService`.

- **Success:** `ShowSuccessAsync(...)` after create / update / delete / toggle (and similar writes).
- **Failure:** `ShowErrorAsync(...)`.
- On `FluentValidation.ValidationException`, join `ex.Errors` → `ErrorMessage` strings into the error dialog.
- Other exceptions: show `ex.Message`.
- Don't rely only on an inline red `TextBlock` for writes.
- Don't show success dialogs for pure reads (`Load` / `Get`) unless clearly needed.
- Inject `IUserDialogService` into ViewModels; keep dialog chrome out of Business.

```csharp
try
{
    await _service.CreateAsync(...);
    await _dialogs.ShowSuccessAsync("Enregistrement réussi.");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    await _dialogs.ShowErrorAsync(
        string.Join(Environment.NewLine, ex.Errors.Select(e => e.ErrorMessage)));
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    await _dialogs.ShowErrorAsync(ex.Message);
}
```

## ViewModel DI lifetimes

- Prefer **Transient** for ViewModels (fresh state per navigation: list/detail, search, form fields).
- Avoid Singleton ViewModels that hold mutable screen state.

**Rule of thumb:** one file per View/ViewModel, thin UI, always dialog feedback on DB writes.
